From afe8874959771bad663d095b2c38cf1f690b659f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:25:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 027/206] test(shorebird): round-trip tests through the real
 encoder and decoder

Extracts WriteToStream(BaseWriteStream*) out of WriteToFile for all three
types, WriteToFile becoming a thin wrapper. The split exists so the round-trip
tests can drive the actual production encoder into memory. Hand-rolling the
encoding in the test would have compared the reader against the test's own copy
of the format and would still have passed if the real writer disagreed --
the one bug a round-trip is for.

Five tests, all field-by-field: every cid/id/name/hash, num_cids for the class
table, max_field_id for the field table, and every (lo, hi) range for the
dispatch table. Each also asserts PendingBytes() == 0, so a decoder that
stopped early or over-read cannot pass. Dispatch rows with zero, one and three
ranges are all covered so an off-by-one in the inner loop cannot survive.
Nothing routes through ShorebirdLinker::shared(); the *LinkInfo objects are
constructed directly, so there is no process-wide state and no ordering
dependency between tests.

Byte-neutrality re-verified on the six fixtures (hello + five variants), three
binary artifacts plus their three JSON twins, from cached identical dills:

  ours pre-D1 baseline vs ours now : 36/36 identical
  ours now             vs theirs   : 24/36 (the same 12 pre-existing
                                     field_table intra-id ordering misses)

So neither D1 nor this extraction changed a single emitted byte, measured
cumulatively against the baseline taken before any of this work started.

Negative control: swapped the name/hash reads in ReadClassTableLinkInfo --
a realistic typo that keeps entry count, the trailing scalar and stream
alignment all perfectly intact. RoundTrip_ClassTableLinkInfo caught it on all
three entries (exit 255) while the trailing-scalar test and the null-path
smoke test both still exited 0. That is the point: the content assertions are
load-bearing, and a count-only or trailer-only suite would have shipped it.

Copyability of the three *LinkInfo types is left unconstrained deliberately,
for lack of evidence -- not a finding that their types are copyable. Noted in
link_info.h rather than encoding a guess as DISALLOW_COPY_AND_ASSIGN.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/shorebird/link_info.cc b/runtime/vm/shorebird/link_info.cc
